-- title:
--   Each branch of the level-zero edge chart is smooth of dimension one
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a commutative ring and $\pi \in \mathcal O$ an element whose principal ideal $(\pi)$ is maximal, and let $r$ be a natural number. Write $A :=$ `chartERing 𝒪 π r`, the localisation of $\mathrm{MvPolynomial}(\mathrm{Fin}\,2, \mathcal O)/(\mathrm{edgeRel}\,\mathcal O\,\pi)$ away from the element `edgeQuot.discr 𝒪 π r` (the class of `edgeDiscr 𝒪 r`), and let $A_0 := A/\big((\text{image of }\pi\text{ in }A)^{0+1}\big)$, the exponent being written $0+1$ as the level-zero case of a general family. Let $P$ be an ideal of $A_0$ which is a minimal prime of $A_0$, i.e. lies in `minimalPrimes A₀`. The assertion is that the morphism of schemes $\operatorname{Spec}(A_0/P) \to \operatorname{Spec}\big(\mathcal O/(\pi^{0+1})\big)$ obtained by applying $\operatorname{Spec}$ to the ring homomorphism $\mathcal O/(\pi^{0+1}) \to A_0/P$ — namely the map induced on quotients by $\mathcal O \to A$ (legitimate since $\pi^{0+1}$ maps into the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ raised to the power $0+1$), followed by the projection $A_0 \to A_0/P$ — satisfies `SmoothOfRelativeDimension 1`.
--
--   The ring $A_0$ is the special fibre, at level zero, of the standard edge chart in the Čerednik–Drinfeld/Mumford construction of the formal model of the $p$-adic upper half plane; its special fibre is a union of two rational branches meeting transversally, and the statement says that each branch, taken with its reduced structure coming from a minimal prime, is smooth of relative dimension $1$ over the residue field $\mathcal O/(\pi)$. It is used in the Mumford-style glueing step, in the construction of a closed immersion of each irreducible component of the special fibre which is integral and smooth of relative dimension one.
-- source:
